Subject: Borrow Fusion For Argument Keys

What this does
  Adds OP_get_arg8_array_el: `get_arg(a) get_array_el` becomes one instruction
  whose KEY is the argument register, borrowed out of arg_buf[a] and never
  pushed. It is the argument-register form of OP_get_loc8_array_el and has the
  same stack effect -- the receiver stays on the operand stack and only the key
  folds in.

Why we need it
  The key-borrow fusion existed for locals only, so `obj[k]` with `k` a function
  parameter paid a second dispatch, a 16-byte stack push and pop for the key, and
  a js_dup / JS_FreeValue round trip on it. Measured here: 2,001,000 executions
  on a lookup-shaped row, 5.3% of its dispatches.

What it adds
  Borrowing the KEY is legal on different terms from the triple's argument, which
  must be owned: JS_GetPropertyValueConst() never frees its prop argument, and
  the only user code it can reach with the key still live takes its own
  reference. It takes the slot of OP_set_var_ref3, which put_short_code() now
  emits only for index 0, so writes to closure variables 1-3 take the general
  3-byte form. The handler body is duplicated rather than shared with the local
  form, so that form's register allocation is unchanged, and it adds no
  function-wide local.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes. `9999` carries the version and the rebuilt built-in blobs.

